      </div>
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p>Across our team, AI is already a powerful partner for boilerplate, quick scripts, exploration, and getting unstuck. But generating code is only one small part of making dependable software. Agents can misunderstand requirements, make confident mistakes, or produce code that looks plausible while quietly ignoring the system around it. Humans still need to set direction, understand the domain, design the architecture, and check that the result is correct, secure, reliable, and—where hardware is involved—safe.</p>
        <p>Over the next 5–10 years, we expect more implementation, testing, deployment, and monitoring to be delegated to increasingly capable agents. Engineers will shift toward specifying what should happen, deciding what is worth building, orchestrating tools, and validating outcomes. In other words: fewer hours typing every line, more responsibility for knowing whether those lines deserve to exist.</p>
        <div class="future-points">
