<body>
  <header class="site-header">
    <div class="wrap header-row">
      <div>
        <h1>Team The Human-in-the-Loop</h1>
        <p class="tagline">Five engineers, one very patient review queue, and a shared belief that “it compiled” is not the same as “it’s correct.”</p>
      </div>
      <button id="theme-toggle" type="button" aria-label="Switch to night mode">
        <span class="toggle-icon" aria-hidden="true">☾</span>
        <span class="toggle-label">Night mode</span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="wrap">
    <section id="team" aria-labelledby="team-heading">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="team-heading">Meet the team</h2>
        <p>Signal processing, cloud workflows, embedded wires, and at least one strong anti-anime position: here are the humans steering the agents.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="member-grid">
        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:gagan.jpeg}}" alt="Gagan, team member">
          <div class="card-content">
            <h3>Gagan</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering at Paderborn University, specializing in signal and information processing; B.Sc. in Electronics and Communication Engineering.</p>
            <p class="bio">Gagan studied electronics and communication engineering before pursuing an M.Sc. in Electrical Systems Engineering at Paderborn University, specializing in signal and information processing. Outside engineering, he enjoys bike rides, music, and fitness. He thinks AI agents already write much of the code, but still struggle to understand requirements and structure software well, so human supervision remains essential. In five to ten years, he expects agents to tackle more complex requests while engineers increasingly review their work and guide faster development.</p>
            <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Gagan hates anime.</em></p>
            <div class="hot-take">🔥 Developers won't be replaced by AI, but developers who don't supervise it well will be replaced by those who do.</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:namit.jpg}}" alt="Namit, team member">
          <div class="card-content">
            <h3>Namit</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science, 5th semester.</p>
            <p class="bio">Namit is a fifth-semester M.Sc. Computer Science student who enjoys football, chess, and vibe-coding random projects just for fun. He sees AI as changing which skills matter: when tools can produce code, engineers add value by deciding what is worth building and designing how its parts fit together. Over the next five to ten years, he expects AI to write most code and engineering work to move closer to product management and architecture. He also thinks people should preserve their own ability to communicate without outsourcing every email to a chatbot.</p>
            <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Namit is left-handed, like roughly one person in ten.</em></p>
            <div class="hot-take">🔥 AI is making us lose our edge: we can't even reply to an email anymore without pasting it into ChatGPT first.</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:ranjith.png}}" alt="Ranjith, team member">
          <div class="card-content">
            <h3>Ranjith</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering (Signal &amp; Information Processing) at Universität Paderborn.</p>
            <p class="bio">Ranjith studies signal and information processing in the M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering program at Universität Paderborn. He enjoys building Python backends and automation, and experimenting with computer vision on real hardware. AI helps him with boilerplate, glue code, and getting unstuck, but he finds it confidently wrong often enough that engineers must understand and review every line. Looking ahead, he expects agents to implement more while engineers focus on specifications, system design, testing, and observability. His projects also explore reinforcement learning for drone navigation.</p>
            <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Ranjith is building a kitchen staff-tracking system with LiDAR instead of cameras, so nobody gets filmed.</em></p>
            <div class="hot-take">🔥 AI won't replace engineers, but it will expose the ones who never understood the code they were copying.</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:sarpesh.jpg}}" alt="Sarpesh, team member">
          <div class="card-content">
            <h3>Sarpesh</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering, 3rd semester, Paderborn University.</p>
            <p class="bio">Sarpesh is a third-semester M.Sc. Electrical Systems Engineering student at Paderborn University. His interests include embedded systems, power electronics, automation, and control systems. He sees AI agents as useful assistants for boilerplate, quick scripts, and explaining unfamiliar code, but says confident mistakes make review and testing essential. In five to ten years, he expects engineers to spend less time typing and more time specifying and verifying agent-produced work. He believes domain knowledge will be especially important when deciding whether generated solutions in embedded and electrical systems are correct and safe.</p>
            <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Sarpesh spends more time debugging wiring than code, and the wiring usually wins.</em></p>
            <div class="hot-take">🔥 An AI that writes code you can't verify isn't saving time; it's just moving the bug to production faster.</div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="member-card">
          <img class="member-photo" src="{{image:soumya.png}}" alt="Soumya Bani, team member">
          <div class="card-content">
            <h3>Soumya Bani</h3>
            <p class="study">M.Sc. Computer Science student at Paderborn University, Germany.</p>
            <p class="bio">Soumya Bani studies Computer Science at Paderborn University and is interested in DevOps, AI, and cloud computing. She explores how AI integration and automation can improve real-world engineering workflows. She sees AI as a productivity-boosting assistant, while emphasizing that architecture, debugging, reliability, and security still require strong engineering fundamentals. Over the next five to ten years, she expects engineers to orchestrate increasingly autonomous agents that build, test, deploy, and monitor applications. Their work will increasingly involve designing systems, setting constraints, and validating outcomes across connected cloud, DevOps, and AI workflows.</p>
            <p class="fun-fact"><em>Fun fact: Soumya is building a multi-agent incident management system to investigate incidents and streamline operational workflows.</em></p>
            <div class="hot-take">🔥 The future belongs to engineers who know what to automate and, more importantly, what not to.</div>
          </div>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="future-of-se" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 id="future-heading">The Future of Software Engineering</h2>
        <p>Our shared take: the job is changing, not disappearing. The keyboard may get less exercise; engineering judgment definitely won't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="future-panel">
        <p>Across our team, AI is already a powerful partner for boilerplate, quick scripts, exploration, and getting unstuck. But generating code is only one small part of making dependable software. Agents can misunderstand requirements, make confident mistakes, or produce code that looks plausible while quietly ignoring the system around it. Humans still need to set direction, understand the domain, design the architecture, and check that the result is correct, secure, reliable, and—where hardware is involved—safe.</p>
        <p>Over the next 5–10 years, we expect more implementation, testing, deployment, and monitoring to be delegated to increasingly capable agents. Engineers will shift toward specifying what should happen, deciding what is worth building, orchestrating tools, and validating outcomes. In other words: fewer hours typing every line, more responsibility for knowing whether those lines deserve to exist.</p>
        <div class="future-points">
          <div class="future-point"><strong>Delegate the typing</strong>Let agents accelerate implementation and repetitive work.</div>
          <div class="future-point"><strong>Keep the judgment</strong>Product thinking, architecture, and domain expertise make output useful.</div>
          <div class="future-point"><strong>Verify the result</strong>Testing, review, observability, reliability, and security are non-negotiable.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer>Built by an agent, supervised by humans. 2026 · The Human-in-the-Loop</footer>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");
      const icon = button.querySelector(".toggle-icon");
      const label = button.querySelector(".toggle-label");

      function updateButton(theme) {
        const switchTo = theme === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        button.setAttribute("aria-label", `Switch to ${switchTo} mode`);
        icon.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "☀" : "☾";
        label.textContent = theme === "dark" ? "Day mode" : "Night mode";
      }

      updateButton(document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme"));

      button.addEventListener("click", () => {
        const nextTheme = document.documentElement.getAttribute("data-theme") === "dark" ? "light" : "dark";
        document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", nextTheme);
        try {
          localStorage.setItem("upb-theme", nextTheme);
        } catch (_) {
          // Theme still changes for this visit if storage is unavailable.
        }
        updateButton(nextTheme);
      });
    })();
  </script>
